{
  "dom_id": "step-q48358",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14197806/assets/dededc3b9924ff278f45a6149a0b7a5ca0464ee2c3b80aa85d12187a86a93725.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,3].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $-1$ and $1$ inclusively, then there exists a number $\\text{ ________ }$ such that $f(c)=k.$*",
  "worked_solution": "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,3]$, the intermediate value theorem states that \n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(-2)$ and $f(3)$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,3]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nFrom the graph we see that $f(-2)=1$ and $f(3)=-1.$Therefore, the missing part is \"$c\\in [-2,3]$\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14197806/assets/85a590ba283dae96329692fee1aa6e08045fb06e5baa8a42d8308fe8c2558f02.png)",
